g-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network integrates all major experimentally supported prebiotic synthetic routes to glycine, reflecting its status as the most robust abiotic amino acid product. The core strategy is to develop parallel pathways from simple feedstocks (HCN, NH3, H2CO, glyoxylate, CO2, CO), leveraging both Strecker/cyanosulfidic (aminoacetonitrile) and reductive amination (glyoxylate) frameworks. Each is built out from mineralogical and environmental plausibility (hydrothermal, surface, biochemical), explicitly modeling the key intermediates (aminoacetonitrile, glyoxylate imine, hydantoin) and feedstock limitations highlighted in the literature. Ten distinct routes are included: aqueous Strecker, cyanosulfidic UV, HCN oligomer hydrolysis, Bucherer\u2013Bergs, hydrothermal Strecker, glyoxylate reductive amination on Fe(II) minerals (including brucite), H2/Ni amination, cyanide-reducing glyoxylate, CO/NiFe catalyzed, and impact chemistry. All environmental and catalytic requirements, as well as major scientific critiques, are encoded.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2CH2CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"aminoacetonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4N2/c3-1-2-4/h1H2,3H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycinamide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H6N2O/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H2,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"HOCH2CN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyacetonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolonitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO/c3-1-2-4/h2H,1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"diaminomaleonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DAMN\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2N4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h3-6H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"N-(hydroxyacetyl)amine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"\u03b1-hydroxyglycine (hemiaminal)\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"iminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine imine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H3NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxyacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h3H,1H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4N2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4N2O2/c6-1-2-3(7)5-4-1/h1-2H2,(H2,4,5,6,7)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_016\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon monoxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO/c1-2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_017\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"water\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"null\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_018\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2S\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"sulfane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen sulfide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2S/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_019\",\n      \"formula\": \"K4[Fe(CN)6]\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"potassium hexacyanoferrate(II)\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ferrocyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_020\",\n      \"formula\": \"NO3-\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"nitrate\",\n      \"common_name\": \"nitrate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/NO3/c2-1(3)4/q-1\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_021\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH2OH\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxylamine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3NO/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker condensation (surface pathway)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"mildly alkaline pH (~8-9), possible wet-dry cycles\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Formaldehyde and ammonia condense to methanimine, which reacts with HCN to give aminoacetonitrile (major) and glycolonitrile (side).\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the classic Strecker scenario, with robust yields under plausible prebiotic conditions if all three reagents are locally concentrated. (Johnson et al. 2008; Cleaves et al. 2008; Miller; Glavin et al. 2025)\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Aminoacetonitrile hydrolysis (to glycinamide)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"hydrolysis, base/acid speed up\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aminoacetonitrile undergoes partial hydrolysis to glycinamide; can be stepwise.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported in Strecker and meteorite systems; this step is robust and needs only modestly alkaline or acid conditions (Miller; Johnson et al. 2008).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycinamide hydrolysis (to glycine)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"hydrolysis, mild acid/base\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrolysis of glycinamide releases glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Spontaneous under surface/aqueous conditions; observed in Strecker and meteorite aqueous alteration runs (Johnson et al. 2008; Glavin et al. 2025).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanosulfidic photoredox (glycolonitrile formation)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_018\", \"mol_019\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I)/Cu(II)\", \"UV\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"UV irradiation (254 nm)\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reductive homologation of HCN with H2S gives glycolonitrile (along with other products) via a photoredox cycle.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by Patel et al. 2015, Sutherland; strongly evidenced, but requires spatial/temporal choreography and local high cyanide (Patel et al. 2015).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolonitrile \u2192 aminoacetonitrile (ammonolysis)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": null\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glycolonitrile is aminated by NH3 to aminoacetonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Confirmed in cyanosulfidic surface reactors, also can occur in aqueous evaporitic pools (Patel et al. 2015; Sutherland; Ferris et al. 1978).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN oligomerization and hydrolysis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"280-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"initial pH 9.2, followed by acid/base hydrolysis\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN oligomerizes to DAMN and various higher oligomers; hydrolysis liberates aminoacetonitrile, glycine, and small amides.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Ferris et al. 1974, 1978; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013: robustly produces glycine, but only from highly concentrated HCN (>0.01 M).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Bucherer\u2013Bergs/hydantoin pathway\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293K (formation), later 353K (hydrolysis)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"sequential steps\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN, H2CO, NH3, and CO2 react by the Bucherer\u2013Bergs pathway to give hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strongly supported (Pulletikurti et al. 2022; Ferris et al. 1974). Connects Strecker/nitrile and metabolic/ketoacid worlds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin hydrolysis (to glycine)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353K (80\u00b0C)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acid or base\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring hydrolyzed, yielding glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Well demnstrated for glycine (Pulletikurti et al. 2022; Ferris et al. 1974).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination of glyoxylate by NH3 (Fe(II) mineral mediated)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 7\u201310\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia reversibly combine to the hemiaminal intermediate (\u03b1-hydroxyglycine), precursor for imine/reductive step.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glyoxylate\u2013ammonia equilibrium is supported (Paczelt et al. 2023; Barge et al. 2020).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine imine formation and reduction (Fe(II) or metallic iron or brucite/green rust)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Fe\u00b2\u207a/Fe(III)/Fe(0), brucite, H2, Ni as reductant, pH 7\u201310\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Dehydration of hemiaminal yields glycine imine, which is then reduced.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Key intermediate in Fe(II)-mediated glycine synthesis (Paczelt et al. 2023; Barge et al. 2020; Chimiak et al. 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive amination to glycine on Fe(II) (hydrothermal/FeS/green rust/brucite)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe\u00b2\u207a\", \"green rust\", \"Fe(0)\", \"ferroan brucite\", null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"293-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1-10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe\u00b2\u207a mineral\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3 or NH4+, mildly alkaline pH\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Fe(II) or Fe(0) reduces the imine to glycine; brucite surface enables the reaction with nitrate/NH4+ coupling.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly evidenced in Barge et al. 2020; Chimiak et al. 2024; Kaur et al. 2024; Paczelt et al. 2023. Glycine formed in situ from glyoxylate imine under these conditions (multiple labs).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"H2/Ni catalyzed glyoxylate reductive amination\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_015\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni\u2070\", null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"295K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"5 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2\u2013Al2O3\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH 8\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate and ammonia react under hydrogen and Ni/SiO2\u2013Al2O3 to afford glycine (1:1 selectivity with glycolate); iron meteorites also tested.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. 2024: experimentally realized with natural iron meteorite as well as Ni on oxide support.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_013\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanide-based reduction of glyoxylate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [null],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanide as reducing agent\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Glyoxylate undergoes reductive amination via cyanide as an electron donor to produce glycine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Supported by Yadav et al. 2022 (Nature Chem 14:170\u2013178); glycine is major product, though this still relies on cyanide presence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_014\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Strecker synthesis\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"pyrite\u2013pyrrhotite\u2013magnetite (redox buffer)\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"423K (150\u00b0C)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"10 bar\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"redox buffer, but not catalyst\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"CO2/H2 buffer\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Strecker-type condensation under hydrothermal constraints; aminoacetonitrile forms efficiently at ~150\u00b0C but is unstable at higher T.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Directly evidenced in Hennet et al. 1992; Andersson & Holm 2000; product accumulates at moderate vent temperatures. Above ~200\u00b0C, amino acids are destroyed (Bada et al. 2009).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_015\",\n      \"name\": \"CO/NiFe surface catalyzed amino acid formation\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_016\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_017\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"NiS\", \"(Ni,Fe)S\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"353-393K (80\u2013120\u00b0C)\",\n        \"pressure\": \"75 bar CO\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"NiS, (Ni,Fe)S\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyano and methylthio ligands\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"CO, HCN, and NH3 react on NiS to yield gl