ases glycine.", "reasoning": "Spontaneous under surface/aqueous conditions; observed in Strecker and meteorite aqueous alteration runs (Johnson et al. 2008; Glavin et al. 2025)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Cyanosulfidic photoredox (glycolonitrile formation)", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002", "mol_018", "mol_019", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_007"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Cu(I)/Cu(II)", "UV"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-320K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Cu(I)/Cu(II) photoredox", "aqueous": true, "other": "UV irradiation (254 nm)"}, "mechanism": "Reductive homologation of HCN with H2S gives glycolonitrile (along with other products) via a photoredox cycle.", "reasoning": "Supported by Patel et al. 2015, Sutherland; strongly evidenced, but requires spatial/temporal choreography and local high cyanide (Patel et al. 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Glycolonitrile \u2192 aminoacetonitrile (ammonolysis)", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": null}, "mechanism": "Glycolonitrile is aminated by NH3 to aminoacetonitrile.", "reasoning": "Confirmed in cyanosulfidic surface reactors, also can occur in aqueous evaporitic pools (Patel et al. 2015; Sutherland; Ferris et al. 1978)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "HCN oligomerization and hydrolysis", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_017"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_004", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [null], "conditions": {"temperature": "280-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "initial pH 9.2, followed by acid/base hydrolysis"}, "mechanism": "HCN oligomerizes to DAMN and various higher oligomers; hydrolysis liberates aminoacetonitrile, glycine, and small amides.", "reasoning": "Ferris et al. 1974, 1978; Ruiz-Bermejo et al. 2013: robustly produces glycine, but only from highly concen