m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: The network integrates all major experimentally supported prebiotic synthetic routes to glycine, reflecting its status as the most robust abiotic amino acid product. The core strategy is to develop parallel pathways from simple feedstocks (HCN, NH3, H2CO, glyoxylate, CO2, CO), leveraging both Strecker/cyanosulfidic (aminoacetonitrile) and reductive amination (glyoxylate) frameworks. Each is built out from mineralogical and environmental plausibility (hydrothermal, surface, biochemical), explicitly modeling the key intermediates (aminoacetonitrile, glyoxylate imine, hydantoin) and feedstock limitations highlighted in the literature. Ten distinct routes are included: aqueous Strecker, cyanosulfidic UV, HCN oligomer hydrolysis, Bucherer\u2013Bergs, hydrothermal Strecker, glyoxylate reductive amination on Fe(II) minerals (including brucite), H2/Ni amination, cyanide-reducing glyoxylate, CO/NiFe catalyzed, and impact chemistry. All environmental and catalytic requirements, as well as major scientific critiques, are encoded.\n\nMOLECULES (21 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_003: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_004: NH2CH2CN (aminoacetonitrile) [