d\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance all reactions (add water for hydrolysis, check for ammonia formation, correct stoichiometry).\",\n        \"Explicitly show the formation and fate of byproducts (e.g., NH3 released in amide hydrolysis).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_004\": 0.4,\n        \"rxn_005\": 0.7,\n        \"rxn_001\": 0.6,\n        \"rxn_002\": 0.8\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.60,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.8,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.8,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.70,\n      \"feedback\": \"This is a variant of the cyanosulfidic photoredox/ammonia pathway, which is well referenced (Sutherland, 2015, and follow-up literature). However, rxn_004 is severely unbalanced in almost every element, and rxn_001 is also unbalanced (as above). Cyanosulfidic chemistry is demonstrated in laboratory flows, but requires sequential delivery of H2S, UV light, and cyanide, and intermediates such as ferrocyanide are not properly conserved as written. Solution: explicitly balance all photoredox and ammonolysis steps and account for all atoms and salts. Mechanistically and environmentally, this is a detailed and realistic scenario that tracks modern experiments, but reaction balancing must be enforced.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_001\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Correct stoichiometry and atom balance for the photoredox step and ammonolysis.\",\n        \"Clarify role and cycling of mineral mediators (ferrocyanide, Fe/Ni complexes).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_006\": 0.8\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.8,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.85,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 1.0,\n      \"target_achievement\": 1.0,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.85,\n      \"feedback\": \"The HCN oligomer hydrolysis pathway is well supported experimentally and aligns with classic Ferris and Ruiz-Bermejo reviews. rxn_006 can be balanced using integer coefficients, supporting conservation. The route is chemically plausible and the environment (surface, pH ~9, aqueous) matches the literature. Some ambiguity remains on co-products, but the conversion to glycine from HCN via oligomers is robust and well established.\",\n      \"approved\": true,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Explicitly enumerate all main oligomer byproducts/co-products for completeness.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_007\": 0.8,\n        \"rxn_008\": 0.4\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.60,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.80,\n      \"environmental_progression\": 0.95,\n      \"target_achievement\": 0.7,\n      \"overall_confidence\": 0.70,\n      \"feedback\": \"The Bucherer\u2013Bergs (hydantoin) pathway is an important and experimentally validated bridge from cyanide chemistry to amino acid formation, but rxn_008 is seriously unbalanced and does not account for the extra C, N, and O present. The initial hydantoin formation is plausible and balanceable. This pathway is mechanistically plausible, but product and co-product accounting (especially in the hydantoin hydrolysis step) must be accurate.\",\n      \"approved\": false,\n      \"problematic_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\"],\n      \"suggestions\": [\n        \"Balance hydantoin hydrolysis to include missing byproducts (e.g., urea, CO2, NH3, or other small molecules).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"reaction_scores\": {\n        \"rxn_009\": 0.6,\n        \"rxn_010\": 0.5,\n        \"rxn_011\": 0.5\n      },\n      \"chemical_feasibility\": 0.55,\n      \"pathway_coherence\": 0.80,\n      \"enviro