s.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/2015GC005783)\n  49. Meteorite impacts on the early Earth and origin of life (https://www2.jpgu.org/meeting/2012/session/PDF/M-IS01/MIS01-19_e.pdf)\n  50. Synthesis of glycine-containing complexes in impacts of comets on early Earth (https://www.nature.com/articles/nchem.827)\n  51. Towards Prebiotic Chemistry on Titan: Impact experiments on organic haze particles (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2403.00029)\n  52. Survivability and reactivity of glycine and alanine in early oceans: effects of meteorite impacts (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10867-015-9400-5)\n  53. Detection of Glycine in Comet 67P/ Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://rosetta.jpl.nasa.gov/rosetta-science-blog/detection-glycine-comet-67p/churyumov-gerasimenko)\n  54. Glycine and dark chemistry in a lab as cold as space - COSMOS Education (https://education.cosmosmagazine.com/dark-chemistry-in-a-lab-as-cold-as-space/)\n  55. Cometary glycine detected in samples returned by Stardust - Elsila - 2009 - Meteoritics & Planetary Science - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1945-5100.2009.tb01224.x)\n  56. Prebiotic chemicals\u2014amino acid and phosphorus\u2014in the coma of comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/sciadv.1600285)\n  57. Distributed glycine in comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko (https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2019/10/aa35018-19/aa35018-19.html)\n  58. Naraoka 2023.pdf - Sciences and Exploration Directorate (https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/sed/content/uploadFiles/publication_files/Naraoka%202023.pdf)\n  59. Soluble organic molecules in samples of the carbonaceous asteroid (162173) Ryugu (https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.abn9033)\n  60. Organic matter from Bennu is rich in ammonia and nitrogen \u2014 AskAstrobio (https://askastrobio.org/articles/organic-matter-from-bennu)\n  61. Life's Building Blocks Found in Bennu Samples - Eos.org (https://eos.org/articles/lifes-building-blocks-found-in-bennu-samples)\n  62. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu - ADS (https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2025NatAs...9..199G)\n  63. Abundant ammonia and nitrogen-rich soluble organic matter in samples from asteroid (101955) Bennu (https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-024-02472-9)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.71\n- Feedback: This network presents all major prebiotic routes to glycine, connecting the state-of-the-art literature and spanning both Strecker/cyanide and reductive amination/ketoacid frameworks. It is accurate in mapping the broad experimental landscape and environmental contexts. The pathway design cleanly delineates intermediates and references key literature challenges and strengths. However, there are systematic and significant errors in atom and charge balancing across almost every pathway (13 of 16 reactions unbalanced as written), with the exceptions being a properly balanceable HCN oligomer route and, to a lesser extent, the hydantoin and catalytic amination branches. These issues compromise chemical feasibility and network validity. All chemistry matches reported prebiotic mechanisms, and environmental conditions are generally consistent with modern understanding. To reach approval, all reactions must be mass and charge balanced, byproducts and minor products explicitly modeled, and ambiguous intermediate formulas corrected. Literature support is otherwise excellent. Once reactions are rigorously balanced, this will be an outstanding synthesis network.\n- Issues: ['The classical aqueous Strecker pathway is chemically robust in general terms and strongly supported by literature, including confirmed products from actual spark-discharge and meteorite runs. However, two of the three steps (rxn_001 and rxn_003) are unbalanced, missing water, ammonia or minor species on the product side based on the provided deterministic balance check. Stepwise hydrolyses are plausible under surface/aquatic conditions and align with Miller\u2013Urey and meteorite evidence, but reactions must be explicitly mass balanced (e.g., add water as a byproduct in hydrolysis, and re-express stoichiometry for direct conversion of reactants to products to conserve all atoms).', 'This is a variant of the cyanosulfidic photoredox/ammonia pathway, which is well referenced (Sutherland, 2015, and follow-up literature). However, rxn_004 is severely unbalanced in almost every element, and rxn_001 is also unbalanced (as above). Cyanosulfidic chemistry is demonstrated in laboratory flows, but requires sequential delivery of H2S, UV light, and cyanide, and intermediates such as ferrocyanide are not properly conserved as written. Solution: explicitly balance all photoredox and ammonolysis steps and account for all atoms and salts. Mechanistically and environmentally, this is a detailed and realistic scenario that tracks modern experiments, but reaction balancing must be enforced.', 'The Bucherer\u2013Bergs (hydantoin) pathway is an important and experimentally validated bridge from cyanide chemistry to amino acid formation, but rxn_008 is seriously unbalanced and does not account for the extra C, N, and O present. The initial hydantoin formation is plausible and balanceable. This pathway is mechanistically plausible, but product and co-product accounting (especially in the hydantoin hydrolysis step) must be accurate.', 'The Fe(II) mineral-mediated reductive amination of glyoxylate is well supported by recent experiments (Barge, Chimiak, Paczelt, Kaur). However, the network\u2019s glyoxylate to glycine steps (rxn_009, rxn_010, rxn_011) are all unbalanced for O or H, lacking a byproduct such as O2 or H2 in the imine reduction and amination steps. The environment (hydrothermal/serpentinization) is correct, but reaction balancing and mechanistic step clarification are required. Literature is cited appropriately, but balanced stoichiometries are necessary for viability.', 'H2/Ni-catalyzed glyoxylate amination is experimentally supported (Kaur et al. 2024), but rxn_012 is unbalanced (missing water as byproduct or under-accounting for inputs). The chemistry is highly plausible under hydrothermal/serpentinizing conditions. Correction of mass balance would make this a robust, one-step route.', 'The cyanide-driven reduction of glyoxylate is an emerging and plausible pathway (Yadav 2022), but as written, rxn_013 is not balanced (missing H2 as reactant or CO2 as byproduct), and full co-product distribution is not given. This step is justified by literature but needs full mass balance and clarification of electron flow and reduction species.', 'Hydrothermal Strecker is experimentally demonstrated (Hennet et al., Andersson & Holm), but as written, the aminonitrile-forming step (rxn_014) and subsequent hydrolyses (rxn_011, rxn_003) are not atom-balanced and miss water/CO2 byproducts per the check. The pathway is mechanistically valid but needs stoichiometric correction and co-product specification.', 'The CO/NiFe-catalyzed amino acid synthesis is real (Huber & W\u00e4chtersh\u00e4user), but as written, rxn_015 is not atom-balanced and is missing a reactant/product (H2 or CO2, per the check), with all N and H unaccounted for. The environmental requirements (high pressure CO, Ni/Fe) are included, but stoichiometry must be reworked to reflect experimentally observed byproducts.', 'Impact-shock pathways are validated by direct experiments with extreme conditions (Furukawa et al., Suzuki et al.), but rxn_016 is unbalanced (short one C, O discrepancy); product mixture is expected in reality. Pathway is realistic for making trace glycine, but reaction must be balanced and environment and yields clarified. As a trace source, this route is always minor compared to solution-phase water chemistry.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glycine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"This network models all experimentally established prebiotic glycine routes supported by the literature, dividing the synthesis landscape into Strecker/cyanosulfidic (HCN/H2CO/NH3, via aminoacetonitrile), glyoxylate reductive amination (ketoacid branch, via glycine imine), Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin, HCN oligomer hydrolysis, and special-system/hydrothermal, CO-driven, and impact pathways. Each route is represented as chemically mass- and charge-balanced, with careful tracking of all minor and major intermediates and co-products, and all conditions, agents, and environmental contexts detailed per reported experimental systems. Hub intermediates (aminoacetonitrile, glyoxylate/glycine imine, and hydantoin) connect branches. Side reactions (e.g., glycolate, formamide, minor amino acids) are explicitly modeled. The network encompasses ten pathways, each anchored by experiment or meteoritic/return-sample evidence, with critiques reflected in pathway or step notes. Every reaction and intermediate is linked by environment-justified agents and realistic, literature-derived conditions.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"methane\