m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Glycine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: This network models all experimentally established prebiotic glycine routes supported by the literature, dividing the synthesis landscape into Strecker/cyanosulfidic (HCN/H2CO/NH3, via aminoacetonitrile), glyoxylate reductive amination (ketoacid branch, via glycine imine), Bucherer\u2013Bergs hydantoin, HCN oligomer hydrolysis, and special-system/hydrothermal, CO-driven, and impact pathways. Each route is represented as chemically mass- and charge-balanced, with careful tracking of all minor and major intermediates and co-products, and all conditions, agents, and environmental contexts detailed per reported experimental systems. Hub intermediates (aminoacetonitrile, glyoxylate/glycine imine, and hydantoin) connect branches. Side reactions (e.g., glycolate, formamide, minor amino acids) are explicitly modeled. The network encompasses ten pathways, each anchored by experiment or meteoritic/return-sample evidence, with critiques reflected in pathway or step notes. Every reaction and intermediate is linked by environment-justified agents and realistic, literature-derived conditions.\n\nMOLECULES (20 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH4 (methane) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: H2 (hydrogen) [starting